ULL,'Sprecher 3879 aus dem Korpus \"Deutsche Mundarten: Zwirner-Korpus\" des AGD am IDS Mannheim','http://hdl.handle.net/10932/00-01B9-100C-05EC-DE01-9',1,1020,7,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(536,NULL,'Sprecher 3887 aus dem Korpus \"Deutsche Mundarten: Zwirner-Korpus\" des AGD am IDS Mannheim','http://hdl.handle.net/10932/00-01B9-100F-E6AC-EE01-7',1,1020,8,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(537,NULL,'Sprecher 2823 aus dem Korpus \"Deutsche Mundarten: Zwirner-Korpus\" des AGD am IDS Mannheim','http://hdl.handle.net/10932/00-01B9-0E0D-3D64-A201-A',1,1020,9,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(538,NULL,'Sprecher 3899 aus dem Korpus \"Deutsche Mundarten: Zwirner-Korpus\" des AGD am IDS Mannheim','http://hdl.handle.net/10932/00-01B9-1014-795D-0601-1',1,1020,10,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(539,NULL,'Sprecher 3891 aus dem Korpus \"Deutsche Mundarten: Zwirner-Korpus\" des AGD am IDS Mannheim','http://hdl.handle.net/10932/00-01B9-1011-FF5C-F601-C',1,1020,11,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(540,NULL,'Sprecher 132 aus dem Korpus \"Deutsche Mundarten: ehemalige deutsche Ostgebiete\" des AGD am IDS Mannheim','http://hdl.handle.net/10932/00-01B8-EEA0-E637-2F01-D',1,1020,12,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(541,NULL,'Sprecher 128 aus dem Korpus \"Deutsche Mundarten: ehemalige deutsche Ostgebiete\" des AGD am IDS Mannheim','http://hdl.handle.net/10932/00-01B8-EEA0-69F7-2701-4',1,1020,13,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(542,NULL,'Sprecher 127 aus dem Korpus \"Deutsche Mundarten: ehemalige deutsche Ostgebiete\" des AGD am IDS Mannheim','http://hdl.handle.net/10932/00-01B8-EEA0-4B17-2501-6',1,1020,14,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(543,NULL,'Sprecher 3888 aus dem Korpus \"Deutsche Mundarten: Zwirner-Korpus\" des AGD am IDS Mannheim','http://hdl.handle.net/10932/00-01B9-1010-3EDC-F001-C',1,1020,15,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(565,NULL,'HuckFinn','https://b2drop.eudat.eu/s/8f6mMASZWW4wDb8',1,1044,3,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(566,NULL,'Frogs French Iduguine Corpus','https://childes.talkbank.org/access/Frogs/French-Duguine.html',1,1048,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(567,NULL,'Basque SotoValle Corpus - 040505','https://vlo.clarin.eu/record/hdl_58_11312_47_c-00026510-1?5-1.-tabs-panel-fieldsTable-documentField-12-values-0-value-link&fqType=collection:or&fq=collection:TalkBank&fqType=languageCode:or&fq=languageCode:code:eus&index=90&count=339',1,1048,1,'HTTP 200/','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(568,NULL,'Basque Luque Corpus - 33cas3','https://childes.talkbank.org/access/Other/Basque/Luque.html',1,1048,2,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(569,NULL,'Haur Hezkuntzako ipuin-bilduma','http://hdl.handle.net/11304/f27f5e92-af01-4a37-a6d9-82cf14afa160',1,1048,3,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(570,'The XRayLab (ISPC-CNR) of Catania (Italy) in the framework of the interdisciplinary project MINIARE (Manuscript Illumination: Non-Invasive Analysis, Research And Expertise) in collaboration with the Fitzwilliam Museum (Department of Manuscripts and Printed Books), the Hamilton Kerr Institute, the University of Cambridge (Department of Chemistry), carried out MA-XRF analyses on illuminated manuscripts (1350 BC - 19th c. AD) of different geographical provenance, by using in-situ an advanced X-ray imaging scanning system (Landis-X), designed and developed in laboratory. The MA-XRF high-resolution distribution images of the chemical elements composing the painting of the manuscripts have provided, in non-invasive way, the identification of the original materials (pigments) used by the artists increasing the knowledge on the artistic, cultural, political, social, technological, and economic aspects in which the miniatures were created. The elemental images also allowed the visualization of the back of the single pages, no more readable because over time they have been detached from their main books and glued on cardboard,t hus helping their collocation in the original volumes.','MA-XRF analyses on illuminated manuscripts','http://ckan.restore.ovi.cnr.it/dataset/ma-xrf-analyses-on-illuminated-manuscripts',1,1049,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(571,NULL,'Imaging Methods and Scanning Applications for Non-Invasive Dendrochronology','https://www.iperionhs.eu/cat/1/tech/13/',1,1050,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(572,NULL,'Macro XRF scanning','https://www.iperionhs.eu/cat/1/tech/18/',1,1050,1,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(573,'Micro X-ray fluorescence (µXRF) is an elemental analysis technique which allows for the examination of very small sample areas. Like conventional XRF instrumentation, micro X-ray fluorescence uses direct X-ray excitation to induce characteristic X-ray fluorescence emission from the sample for elemental analysis. Unlike conventional XRF, which has a typical spatial resolution ranging in diameter from several hundred micrometers up to several millimeters, µXRF uses X-ray optics to restrict the excitation beam size or focus the excitation beam to a small spot on the sample surface so that small features on the sample can be analyzed. Possibility to map large surfaces (over 10x10 cm2).','Micro X-ray fluorescence mapping (μXRF)','https://doi.org/10.34733/vc-1049',1,1050,2,'HTTP 200/','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(574,NULL,'NIR hyperspectral imaging (900-2500 nm)','https://www.iperionhs.eu/cat/1/tech/16/',1,1050,3,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(575,NULL,'Reflection VIS hyperspectral imaging (400-1000 nm)','https://www.iperionhs.eu/cat/1/tech/12/',1,1050,4,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(576,NULL,'Scanning VIS-NIR multispectral reflectography (395 – 2500 nm)','https://www.iperionhs.eu/cat/1/tech/14/',1,1050,5,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(577,NULL,'Thermography (SIRT)','https://www.iperionhs.eu/cat/1/tech/15/',1,1050,6,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(578,NULL,'UV-VIS induced fluorescence hyperspectral imaging (450-1000 nm)','https://www.iperionhs.eu/cat/1/tech/11/',1,1050,7,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(579,NULL,'XRD mapping','https://www.iperionhs.eu/cat/1/tech/30/',1,1050,8,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(580,NULL,'XRF confocal mapping','https://www.iperionhs.eu/cat/1/tech/33/',1,1050,9,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(581,NULL,'2D/3D ANALYSIS','https://www.iperionhs.eu/category/2/',1,1051,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(582,NULL,'MULTI/HYPER SPECTRAL IMAGING/MAPPING','https://www.iperionhs.eu/category/1/',1,1051,1,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(583,NULL,'POINT ANALYSIS','https://www.iperionhs.eu/category/31/',1,1051,2,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(584,NULL,'REMOTE SENSING','https://www.iperionhs.eu/category/36/',1,1051,3,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(585,'The Acquis Communautaire (AC) is the total body of European Union (EU) law applicable in the the EU Member States. This collection of legislative text changes continuously and currently comprises selected texts written between the 1950s and now. As of the beginning of the year 2007, the EU had 27 Member States and 23 official languages. The Acquis Communautaire texts exist in these languages, although Irish translations are not currently available. The Acquis Communautaire thus is a collection of parallel texts in the following 22 languages: Bulgarian, Czech, Danish, German, Greek, English, Spanish, Estonian, Finnish, French, Hungarian, Italian, Lithuanian, Latvian, Maltese, Dutch, Polish, Portuguese, Romanian, Slovak, Slovene and Swedish.\n\nThe data release by the JRC is in line with the general effort of the European Commission to support multilingualism, language diversity and the re-use of Commission information.\n\nThe Language Technology group of the European Commission\'s Joint Research Centre did not receive an authoritative list of documents that belong to the Acquis Communautaire. In order to compile the document collection distributed here, we selected all those CELEX documents (see below) that were available in at least ten of the twenty EU-25 languages (the official languages of the EU before Bulgaria and Romania joined in 2007) and that additionally existed in at least three of the nine languages that became official languages with the Enlargement of the EU in 2004 (i.e. Czech, Estonian, Hungarian, Lithuanian, Latvian, Maltese, Polish, Slovak and Slovene). The collection distributed here is thus an approximation of the Acquis Communautaire which we call the JRC-Acquis. The JRC-Acquis must not be seen as a legal reference corpus. Instead, the purpose of the JRC-Acquis is to provide a large parallel corpus of documents for (computational) linguistics research purposes.\n\nTerms and conditions: https://ec.europa.eu/jrc/en/language-technologies/jrc-acquis#Usage conditions / Licensing issues','JRC-Acquis Multilingual Parallel Corpus','http://urn.fi/urn:nbn:fi:lb-20140730162',1,1052,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(586,'The data will be available in the Language Bank service download (korp.csc.fi/download). It is available for research purposes until 23 December 21 2023. The user shall commit to removing the downloaded resource from his/her devices and other storage facilities governed by the user on or before 21 December 2023.\n\nThis data set includes an analysis of the original, English-language version and of the Dutch-language version (as released in the Netherlands) of the nine songs with lyrics from the Disney film Frozen. This analysis employs the triangle of aspects, an analytical model developed specifically for translation research into songs from musical films. The collection of these data is part of the licensor’s Ph. D. project, tentatively titled “Musical, visual and verbal aspects of animated film song dubbing: A case study of Disney’s Frozen” (projected for publication in early 2020). This data set comprises 9 PDF files, one for each song, as well as a Word document that summarises the findings and provides copyright notices.\n\nThe data size is 26.6 MB \n\nNote that the resource shall be removed from the CLARIN Service on 21 December 2023. This time limit shall be conveyed to users of the resource upon downloading, and the user shall commit to removing the downloaded resource from his/her devices and other storage facilities governed by the user on or before 21 December 2023. See the Licence at http://urn.fi/urn:nbn:fi:lb-2019040802','Triangle of Aspects Analysis of Frozen','http://urn.fi/urn:nbn:fi:lb-2019022701',1,1052,1,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(587,'The Europarl parallel corpus is extracted from the proceedings of the European Parliament. It includes versions in 21 European languages: Romanic (French, Italian, Spanish, Portuguese, Romanian), Germanic (English, Dutch, German, Danish, Swedish), Slavik (Bulgarian, Czech, Polish, Slovak, Slovene), Finni-Ugric (Finnish, Hungarian, Estonian), Baltic (Latvian, Lithuanian), and Greek. \n\nThe goal of the extraction and processing was to generate sentence aligned text for statistical machine translation systems. For this purpose we extracted matching items and labeled them with corresponding document IDs. Using a preprocessor we identified sentence boundaries. We sentence aligned the data using a tool based on the Church and Gale algorithm.','Europarl Parallel Corpus','http://urn.fi/urn:nbn:fi:lb-20140730195',1,1052,2,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(588,'The Spoken Wikipedia project unites volunteer readers of Wikipedia\n          articles. Hundreds of spoken articles in multiple languages are\n          available to users who are – for one reason or another – unable or\n          unwilling to consume the written version of the article. Our\n          resource, the Spoken Wikipedia Corpus, consolidates the Spoken\n          Wikipediae, adding text segmentation, normalization, time-alignment\n          and further annotations, making it accessible for research and\n          fostering new ways of interacting with the material.','The Spoken Wikipedia Corpora','http://hdl.handle.net/11022/0000-0007-C640-1',1,1052,3,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(589,'The SoNaR Corpus is a large corpus of written Dutch. The corpus consists of two datasets, viz. SoNaR-500 and SoNaR-1.\n                        SONAR-500 contains over 500 million words (i.e. word tokens) of full texts from a wide variety of text types. SONAR-500 does not contain new media texts like tweets, chats and sms.\n                        All texts have been tokenized, tagged for part of speech and lemmatized, while in the same set the Named Entities have been labelled. In the case of SoNaR-500 all annotations were produced automatically, no manual verification took place.  \n                        SoNaR-1 is a dataset comprising one million words. Although largely a subset of SoNaR-500, SoNaR-1 includes far fewer text types. With SoNaR-1 different types of semantic annotation have been provided, viz. named entity labelling, annotation of co-reference relations, semantic role labelling\n                        and annotation of spatial and temporal relations. All annotations have been manually verified.','SoNaR: STEVIN Nederlandstalig Referentiecorpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/87863238c022f388d6b3b5fab0c56fe5',1,1052,4,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(590,'The D-TUNA Corpus is a semantically and pragmatically transparent corpus consisting of 2400 written and (transcribed) spoken Dutch referring expressions.','D-TUNA dataset of Dutch referring expressions','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/6c4a6452708d00a6f7adfe0e63b46e1b',1,1052,5,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(591,'Moroccorp is a corpus of computer-mediated communication in Dutch by Moroccan-Dutch language users, consisting of ten million words of chat material. The data is delivered in a .txt file of 82.4 Mb.','Moroccorp','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/c6473132dfaa29848d109f9955c55545',1,1052,6,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(592,'DPC contains the language pairs Dutch-English and Dutch-French, with Dutch as the central language. A part of the corpus is trilingual.','DPC-corpus: Dutch Parallel Corpus(non-commercial)','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/d68c4ff787acfee15bff623561caf23b',1,1052,7,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(593,'The CD-ROM Middle Dutch (first released in 1998) contains the Middle Dutch Dictionary, the texts from the CorpusGysseling and a collection of almost 300 poetry and prose texts. For reason that the software for installation is no longer compatible with modern operating systems the application will be distributed by the TST-Centrale. This application will only work under Windows.','CDROM Middelnederlands','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/7ce97fbbb8985c8f17df9930d013fea1',1,1052,8,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(594,'The SumNL Corpus of Summaries is based on 30 clusters. Each cluster consists of a subject and 5-25 newspaper articles who are relevant to that subject. Two summaries (of different size) were made for each cluster and also extracts which consist of ten sentences from the texts.','SumNL-samenvattingencorpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/585cf5ed857b25f4e3b2b9aa4a522940',1,1052,9,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(595,'Not all recordings were annotated. For the categories SV, DKR, FT, SS and SSS no annotations were  made.','The Dutch Corpus of Pathological and Normal Speech (COPAS)','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/49e4cf2ce240b8d4eb3cbceab563b8da',1,1052,10,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(596,'Some annotations are only available for part of the corpus.','Corpus Gesproken Nederlands','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/79e296d6905125bc219834ce76d75efb',1,1052,11,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(597,'Only for those words the child hesitates or makes a reading error, labels of oral reading strategies and errors were added.','CHOREC-spraakcorpus (Children\'s Oral Reading Corpus)','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/998e5aeb1e05bded9e49fe9a684537c9',1,1052,12,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(598,'Supervising organisation of the original project','Eindhoven Corpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/9d837b186269e7af25a17f356c558587',1,1052,13,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(599,'The SoNaR New Media Corpus 1.0 consists of the text categories WR-P-E-L_tweets, WR-U-E-A_chats, WR-U-E-D_sms and contains over 35 million words. Those texts were collected within the STEVIN project SoNaR. All texts were tokenised, POS-tagged and lemmatised.','SoNaR: STEVIN Nederlandstalig Referentiecorpus - Nieuwe Media Corpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/60195d484591490edf259ec156ad7aa9',1,1052,14,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(600,'The Wablieft Corpus contains the digital archive of the Wablieft paper (from 2011-2017), as it was published on the website http://www.wablieft.be/krant/archief.It contains two million words from newspaper articles in easy to read Dutch. Metadata is available describing the subject (domestic news, sport, etc.) and date of publication. It comprises all material since the paper was online available in digital form from 2011 until December 2017. The data is available in several formats: original text, split according to sentence, annotated with Frog (POS-tag, lemma, morphology, named-entity, chunking, dependencies relations) in FoLiA and CONNL and syntactically analysed with the Alpino-parser in Alpino-xml.','Wablieft Corpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/c8fe678faddf2e59cced0adf2aef6afe',1,1052,15,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(601,'Not all documentation is available in both languages.','COREA-coreferentiecorpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/ae24b3e3d2da92f4654ada13fcf8573a',1,1052,16,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(602,'An annotation layer which describes phenomena that are characteristic for man-machine interaction, e.g. restarts, fillers, selftalk,...','JASMIN Speech Corpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/10e23ed5a6dfc20c47063c1f5f7316cf',1,1052,17,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(603,'Some annotations are only available for part of the corpus.','Annotations of the Spoken Dutch Corpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/c2f34342379301208058c3a320288e5d',1,1052,18,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(604,'Phonemic labeling and segmentation: defining the phoneme (phoneme transcription) and marking the start and end point of each phoneme (segmentation).','IFA Dutch Spoken Language Corpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/6fe1e37f2ead3834b37b5c922af37e1f',1,1052,19,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(605,'The Boarnsterhim Corpus consists of 250 hours of speech in both West Frisian and Dutch by the same sample of bilingual speakers. The corpus contains original recordings from 1982-1984 and a replication study recorded 35 years later. The data collection spans speech of four generations, and combines panel and trend data.','Boarnsterhim Corpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/0ed521c9fc2d66f870b00aba73e5bebc',1,1052,20,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(606,'Manually annotated gaze direction.','IFA Dialogue Video Corpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/3f681de884473fc4504569ec2b7f3e46',1,1052,21,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(607,'The SumNL Corpus of Summaries (Commercial) is based on 28 clusters. Each cluster consists of a subject and 5-25 newspaper articles who are relevant to that subject. Two summaries (of different size) were made for each cluster and also extracts which consist of ten sentences from the texts.','SumNL-samenvattingencorpus Commercieel','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/29a2f8581c2b7f8a9664a45d90492e55',1,1052,22,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(608,'Multilingual (CEF languages) corpus acquired from website (https://ec.europa.eu/*coronavirus-response) of the EU portal (20th May 2020). It contains 23 TMX files (EN-X, where X is a CEF language) with 53311 TUs in total.','COVID-19 EC-EUROPA v1 dataset. Multilingual (CEF languages)','https://hdl.handle.net/21.11129/0000-000D-FE59-2',1,1052,23,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(609,'Multilingual (CEF languages) corpus acquired from website (https://ec.europa.eu/commission/presscorner/) of the EU portal (8th July 2020). It contains 23 TMX files (EN-X, where X is a CEF language) with 151895 TUs in total.','COVID-19 EU presscorner v2 dataset. Multilingual (CEF languages)','https://hdl.handle.net/21.11129/0000-000D-FE61-8',1,1052,24,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(610,'This dataset has been created within the framework of the European Language Resource Coordination (ELRC) Connecting Europe Facility - Automated Translation (CEF.AT) action. For further information on the project: http://lr-coordination.eu.\nLetter of rights for persons arrested on the basis of a European Arrest Warrant (EAW), 1 page, (Processed)',' Letter of rights for persons arrested on the basis of a European Arrest Warrant (Processed)','https://hdl.handle.net/21.11129/0000-000D-FAD2-C',1,1052,25,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(611,'Multilingual (CEF languages) corpus acquired from the website https://antibiotic.ecdc.europa.eu/ . It contains 20981 TUs (in total) for EN-X language pairs, where X is a CEF language.','COVID-19 ANTIBIOTIC dataset. Multilingual (CEF languages)','https://hdl.handle.net/21.11129/0000-000D-FE5B-0',1,1052,26,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(612,'Multilingual (CEF languages) corpus acquired from website (https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content) of the EU portal (9th July 2020). It contains 23 TMX files (EN-X, X is a CEF language) with 475,931 translation units pairs in total.','COVID-19 EUR-LEX dataset . Multilingual (CEF languages)','https://hdl.handle.net/21.11129/0000-000D-FE69-0',1,1052,27,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(613,'Multilingual (CEF languages) corpus acquired from website (https://ec.europa.eu/commission/presscorner/) of the EU portal (14th May 2020). It contains 23 TMX files (EN-X, where X is a CEF language) with 83217 TUs in total.','COVID-19 EU presscorner v1 dataset. Multilingual (CEF languages)','https://hdl.handle.net/21.11129/0000-000D-FE5E-D',1,1052,28,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(614,'TEI','Corpus Middelnederlands','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/02525b5274ed043b275b4193c88a87c8',1,1052,29,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(615,'sentiment: negative, neutral, positive','DuOMAn Subjectivity Lexicon','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/3baf9acf44f95763603f9b71e389a9a8',1,1052,30,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(616,'This annotation is a manually created broad phonetic transcription (phonemic transcription) including syllable boundaries and stressmarkers.','AUTONOMATA Spoken Names Corpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/f28447cfbfcc9a7e870914ce986398ba',1,1052,31,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(617,'of the CoLing Workshop: Multiword Expressions: From Theory to Applications (MWE 2010)','Paco-MT Parallelle Corpora','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/3564ec9e4c0a4581b1c30fccbeac3dd0',1,1052,32,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(618,'Not all documentation is available in both languages.','Letters as Loot','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/a54c4d368a010bbb5088cc05ca027894',1,1052,33,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(619,'The Basiscript Corpus contains 9 million written words produced by pupils in Dutch elementary school. The corpus contains longitudinal data collected over 3 consecutive years (fall 2012 till spring 2015). Basiscript Corpus has been designed for comparison of educative diversity (types of school), as well as geographical regions of the Netherlands. The data contains mainly hand written texts and a small subset produced with a word processor (proofing tools disabled). The data has been anonimized.','Basiscript Corpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/a07e94033bd5ed49e4a68b5784cbb2ba',1,1052,34,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(620,'The Basilex Corpus is a collection of annotated texts written for children of age 6-12 (primary school). The corpus contains 13.5 million tokens, of which 11.5 million are words. The token are for about 40% derived from educative materials, for about 40% from children literature and 20% from media.','Basilex Corpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/2bb9796b33a5ba6b172d52969e5c5043',1,1052,35,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(621,'This annotation is a manually created broad phonetic transcription (phonemic transcription) including syllable boundaries and stressmarkers.','AUTONOMATA POI Corpus','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/57fd1ff7b5ddbdd7f7731fe613b35fd7',1,1052,36,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(622,'The product Multilingual Subtitle Data 2BDutch consists subtitle data belonging to the Dutch videos on the website www.2BDutch.nl. That website presents videos which can display subtitles in different languages. These videos enable students of Dutch to practice their listening skills and to learn new Dutch words. The subtitles can be used for different applications in the field of language technology like automatic translation and automatic speech recognition.','Meertalige Ondertiteldata 2BDutch','http://hdl.handle.net/10032/149df16305c6abba9c68089a2515abb6',1,1052,37,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(623,'The Stukker_daardoor subcorpus was compiled for a study of the causal connectives \'daardoor\', \'daarom\' and \'dus\' in Dutch news. It consists of cases from a Dutch newspaper (Trouw) from 2001.','Stukker corpus of daardoor','https://hdl.handle.net/1839/00-85E99D3F-55E1-4886-84EB-7AF40ED3A31D',0,1052,38,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(624,'The Pit totadow subcorpus was compiled for a study of the causal connectives \'aangezien\', \'doordat\', \'omdat\' and \'want\' in Dutch, German and French narratives and news. It consists of cases from a Dutch newspaper (de Volkskrant) from 1995.','Pit_news corpus of aangezien, doordat, omdat and want','https://hdl.handle.net/1839/00-196A81B7-6396-4B66-BFCD-2A9CE9C88CF9',0,1052,39,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(625,'The Degand 2.2 subcorpus was compiled for a study of the causal connectives \'aangezien\', \'want\' and \'omdat\' in Dutch news. It consists of 143 cases from a Dutch newspaper (NRC Handelsblad from 1994).','Degand 2.2','https://hdl.handle.net/1839/00-69C7E1DF-8206-46FF-8369-3506235BE319',0,1052,40,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(626,'The SandersSpooren subcorpus was compiled for a study of the causal connectives \'want\' and \'omdat\' in several types of discourse. It consists of cases from news, spontaneous conversation and chat data.','SandersSpooren','https://hdl.handle.net/1839/00-58F6586A-55F4-4B45-8341-6E2F7FF0668C',0,1052,41,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(627,'The Stukker_dus subcorpus was compiled for a study of the causal connectives \'daardoor\', \'daarom\' and \'dus\' in Dutch news. It consists of cases from a Dutch newspaper (Trouw) from 2001.','Stukker corpus of dus','https://hdl.handle.net/1839/00-EEB7D731-9C86-4357-A20F-C5731A17E9C6',0,1052,42,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(628,'The Pit narrdow subcorpus was compiled for a study of the causal connectives \'aangezien\', \'doordat\', \'omdat\' and \'want\' in Dutch, German and French narratives and news. It consists of cases from 22 Dutch novels published between 1990 and 1996.','Pit_narrative corpus of doordat, omdat and want','https://hdl.handle.net/1839/00-90C17E44-FFE2-4919-B55B-45A1F25DB69F',0,1052,43,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(629,'The Persoon subcorpus was compiled for a study of the causal connectives \'want\' and \'omdat\' in Dutch spontaneous conversations. It consists of cases from the Corpus of Spoken Dutch (Corpus Gesproken Nederlands, CGN).','Persoon','https://hdl.handle.net/1839/00-F7414621-69AC-4AF6-9521-32F23A69043E',0,1052,44,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(630,'The PanderMaatSanders subcorpus was compiled for a study of the causal connectives \'daardoor\', \'daarom\' and \'dus\' in Dutch news. It consists of 100 cases from a Dutch newspaper (de Volkskrant  1994 and 1995).','PanderMaatSanders','https://hdl.handle.net/1839/00-56657635-3608-495D-9F4A-1F3F9EA58108',0,1052,45,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(631,'The Stukker_daarom subcorpus was compiled for a study of the causal connectives \'daardoor\', \'daarom\' and \'dus\' in Dutch news. It consists of cases from a Dutch newspaper (Trouw) from 2001.','Stukker corpus of daarom','https://hdl.handle.net/1839/00-BDA159A4-FBBF-4FFB-B5C9-5D8F7BC2EEB0',0,1052,46,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(632,NULL,'Viscosity of alumina doped soda lime silicate glasses','https://doi.org/10.5878/e0r5-d487',1,1053,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(633,'www_mpi_nl','TNd050221-01','https://hdl.handle.net/1839/00-0000-0000-0005-9756-6',0,1054,0,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(634,'027-JT performs a traditional dance of the (male?) ostrich, accompanied by throat singing. Other men (from Corridor 15) did not know or did not want to participate in the first video clip, 027-JT is practicing the dance with (085-AJ?), outside the video capture. 027-JT then decided to perform the dance alone. The video capture is out of focus (CN\'s mistake), that\'s why the dance was repeated in the next session (cf. see also session TWd080413-03).','TWd080413-02','https://hdl.handle.net/1839/00-0000-0000-0009-3DA3-9',0,1054,1,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(635,'656-KP wanted to be recorded playing his guitar.','TWd080413-01','https://hdl.handle.net/1839/00-0000-0000-0009-3DA2-B',0,1054,2,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(636,'The women perform a couple of different dances/plays. They wanted to be recorded. Dancers/Players: 012-AK (red skirt), Katrina Skeppis (pink sweater), 216-AG (black trousers?). Clapping/singing: 268-FT (dress with pink flowers), 138-CG (greenish trousers?), 227-GG (blue trousers), 219-FG (pale reddish skirt).','TWd080413-04','https://hdl.handle.net/1839/00-0000-0000-0009-3DA5-9',0,1054,3,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(659,NULL,'ELTeC','https://github.com/COST-ELTeC/ELTeC-spa',1,1058,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(660,NULL,'CONSSA','https://github.com/cligs/conssa',1,1058,1,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',1,NULL,NULL),(661,NULL,'Audio sobre novelas','http://contenidosdigitales.uned.es/fez/view/intecca:VideoCMAV-5a6f2b5bb1111f57648b4cb3',1,1058,2,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',2,NULL,NULL),(662,NULL,'Audio sobre novelas','http://contenidosdigitales.uned.es/fez/view/intecca:VideoCMAV-5a6f2b5bb1111f57648b4cb3',1,1058,2,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',2,NULL,NULL),(663,'Word embeddings trained on the OpenSubtitles and Wikipedia corpora.',NULL,'https://hdl.handle.net/1839/4f12ff84-f7f5-40e2-9170-79109b39f4d1',0,1059,0,'HTTP 200/','application/x-cmdi+xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(664,NULL,'T1-unamuno','https://drive.google.com/file/d/14dk6dehlya0DQstq-Cn1fq4dobBAVM8o/view?usp=sharing',1,1058,3,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',3,NULL,NULL),(665,NULL,'T2-Fernan-Caballero','https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OHxnBaD-E93W1JwpioYucvYB4r08TsGH/view?usp=sharing',1,1058,4,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',4,NULL,NULL),(666,NULL,'Taxi-1','https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ghFV0fUBR7lEqqnJ2SAiyseGiGcDwZOi/view?usp=sharing',1,1058,5,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',5,NULL,NULL),(667,NULL,'Taxi-2','https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-WoJf1CxdWiJrs52yTZyXwNeAA9Y7PJL/view?usp=sharing',1,1058,6,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',6,NULL,NULL),(668,NULL,'T1-unamuno','https://drive.google.com/file/d/14dk6dehlya0DQstq-Cn1fq4dobBAVM8o/view?usp=sharing',1,1058,3,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',3,NULL,NULL),(669,NULL,'T2-Fernan-Caballero','https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OHxnBaD-E93W1JwpioYucvYB4r08TsGH/view?usp=sharing',1,1058,4,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',4,NULL,NULL),(670,NULL,'Taxi-1','https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ghFV0fUBR7lEqqnJ2SAiyseGiGcDwZOi/view?usp=sharing',1,1058,5,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',5,NULL,NULL),(671,NULL,'Taxi-2','https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-WoJf1CxdWiJrs52yTZyXwNeAA9Y7PJL/view?usp=sharing',1,1058,6,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',6,NULL,NULL),(672,NULL,'Unamuno','https://b2drop.eudat.eu/s/dLBDMkDBrs3HFT7',1,1058,3,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',7,NULL,NULL),(673,NULL,'Caballero','https://b2drop.eudat.eu/s/Hz5cP4aip5wobDP',1,1058,4,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',8,NULL,NULL),(674,'Denna studie söker utveckla en metod för att identifiera förekomster och proportioner av forskare, media- och andra professionella aktiva i Twitterdiskussioner. Som ett fallexempel används ett anonymiserat dataset från vaccindiskussioner på Twitter. Studien föreslår en metod som använder nyckelord som strängar inom listor för att identifiera klasser ifrån användarbiografier. Detta möjliggör en applicering av multipla klassifikationsprinciper till en mängd Twitterbiografier genom att använda semantiska regler genom programmeringsspråket Python. Det skript som använts för att genomföra studien är här deponerat.','Developing a rule-based method for identifying researchers on Twitter: The case of vaccine discussions','https://doi.org/10.5878/akmc-va16',1,1053,1,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'http://localhost',NULL),(677,'The CLARIN infrastructure gives users access to an increasingly rich set of language-related re- sources, using the Virtual Language Observatory, the Federated Content Search, and the Virtual Collection Registry. While there is ample support for searching resources using metadata-based search, or full-text search, or aggregating resources into virtual collections, there is little support for users to help them processing resources in one way or another. While there is a consider- able number of processing software in the CLARIN world, there is no single point of access where users can find tools to fit their needs and the resource they have. In this paper, we present the CLARIN Language Resource Switchboard (LRS), which aims at helping users to connect resources with the tools that can process them. The LRS lists all applicable tools for a given resource, lists the tasks the tools can achieve, and invokes the selected tool in such a way so that processing can start immediately without any or little prior tool parameterization.','Zinn, C. 2016. The CLARIN Language Resource Switchboard. In Proceedings of the CLARIN Annual Conference, Aix-en-Provence, France.','https://www.clarin.eu/sites/default/files/zinn-CLARIN2016_paper_26.pdf',1,1061,0,'HTTP 200/OK','application/pdf',0,NULL,NULL),(678,'The Language Resource Switchboard is becoming a central pillar in the CLARIN infrastruc- ture as it helps researchers to connect resources with tools that can process them in one way or another. Languages resources can be found in different places, and ideally, the switchboard is available nearby. Resources located at users’ desktop computers can simply be uploaded to the switchboard, and resources found in CLARIN’s Virtual Language Observatory can simply be sent to the switchboard by a simple click. Until now, the switchboard was only indirectly accessible for resources stored in the cloud. Here, users had to download a resource from their cloud storage to their desktop device before uploading it again to the switchboard to find appli- cable tools, which is tedious. In this paper, we describe how we linked EUDAT’s B2DROP cloud service to the switchboard, giving users the capability to directly launch the switchboard with a resource from their B2DROP account. Also, we describe the usage of B2DROP to support the switchboard’s back-end for intermediate file storage. The reported work makes a link to another infrastructure, and hence, facilitates and promotes the provision of complementary services to CLARIN members. We believe the cooperation between CLARIN and EUDAT to be of mutual benefit. On the one hand, our bridge makes the use of the generic cloud storage service from EUDAT more attractive to CLARIN members so that they are encouraged to use B2DROP rather than another cloud provider. On the other hand, it encourages EUDAT users to try out and profit from the CLARIN tool space, which in turn will challenge the tool providers to cope with an increased demand, and potentially new user requirements.','Zinn, C. 2018. A Bridge from EUDAT’s B2DROP cloud service to CLARIN’s Language Resource Switchboard. Linköping Electronic Press, (147):36–45.','https://ep.liu.se/ecp/147/004/ecp17147004.pdf',1,1061,1,'HTTP 200/OK','application/pdf',1,NULL,NULL),(679,'The CLARIN research infrastructure gives users access to an increasingly rich and diverse set of language-related resources and tools. Whereas there is ample support for searching resources using metadata-based search, or full-text search, or for aggregating resources into virtual col- lections, there is little support for users to help them process resources in one way or another. In spite of the large number of tools that process texts in many different languages, there is no single point of access where users can find tools to fit their needs and the resources they have. In this squib, we present the Language Resource Switchboard (LRS), which helps users to discover tools that can process their resources. For this, the LRS identifies all applicable tools for a given resource, lists the tasks the tools can achieve, and invokes the selected tool in such a way so that processing can start immediately with little or no prior tool parameterization.','Zinn, C. 2018. The Language Resource Switchboard. Computational Linguistics, 44(4), pages 631-639, December 2018.','https://direct.mit.edu/coli/article/44/4/631/1616/The-Language-Resource-Switchboard',1,1061,2,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',2,NULL,NULL),(680,'Datamaterialet består av 36 ljudinspelningar med totalt 27 informanter/deltagare som alla är tvåspråkiga ngoni från två byar utanför Songea i Ruvumaregionen i Tanzania. Detta ljudmaterial består av 9 fokusgruppdiskussioner och 27 intervjuer med deltagarna. Alla medverkande har givit sitt tillstånd till att materialet kan användas för forskningsändamål.\nFokusgruppinspelningarna är totalt ca 6 timmar. Varje deltagare i dessa fokusgrupperna har dessutom intervjuats enskild efter fokusgruppinspelningen. Alla fokusgruppinspelningarna är transkriberade och sedan översatta till swahili och engelska. Kodväxling till swahili är markerad med fetstil i transkriptioner och översättningar. Intervjuerna genomfördes på swahili, ibland med inslag av ngoni om informanterna behövde det för att förstå. Den engelska översättningen av intervjuerna finns i datasetet, samt ljudinspelningen.\nProjektets syfte var att studera varför människor i Ruvuma-regionen i sydvästra Tanzania kodväxlar, dvs alternerar mellan två koder, swahili och ngoni, som är modersmål för de flesta som bor i området. En huvudfråga var om växlingen mellan ngoni och swahili är ett uttryck för att talare av ngoni inte längre kan uttrycka sig tillräckligt bra på sitt modersmål eller om deras kodväxling signalerar något annat. Kan det exempelvis vara ett sätt att uttrycka gruppidentitet - eller rent av flera identiteter samtidigt? Inom ramen för projektet låg fokus på hur ngoni-talare kodväxlar och om det i språksituationen finns processer som bidrar till bevarandet av modersmålet och om hur ngonitalarens attityder påverkar språkanvändning.\nI inspelningarna talat ngoni , de flesta självhushållsbönde, på ngoni om kulturarv och förändringar. Teman som diskuteras är bl. a ritualer, regler och traditioner, samt namngivning.\n\nDatamaterialet består av 36 ljudinspelningar med totalt 27 informanter/deltagare som alla är tvåspråkiga ngoni (ngoni-swahili) från två byar utanför Songea i Ruvumaregionen i Tanzania, Mhepai och Peramiho B. Detta ljudmaterial består av 9 fokusgruppdiskussioner och 27 intervjuer med deltagarna i fokusgruppsamtalen. Alla medverkande har givit sitt tillstånd till att materialet kan användas för forskningsändamål.\nFokusgruppinspelningarna utgör totalt ca 6 timmar. De 9 fokusgrupperna bestod av: 15 unga mellan 15-20 år (6 unga män och 3 unga kvinnor från Peramiho B samt 3 unga män och 3 unga kvinnor från Mhepai, och 12 personer äldre än 60år (3 äldre kvinnor och tre äldre män från varje by).\n\nVarje deltagare i dessa fokusgrupper har dessutom intervjuats enskild efter fokusgruppinspelningen. Alla fokusgruppinspelningarna är transkriberade och sedan översatta till swahili och engelska. Kodväxling till swahili är markerad med fetstil i transkriptioner och översättningar. Intervjuerna genomfördes på swahili, ibland med inslag av ngoni om informanterna behövde det för att förstå. Den engelska översättningen av intervjuerna finns i datasetet, samt ljudinspelningen. Inspelningarna gjordes in augusti-september 2014.\nEn Excel-fil med anonymiserad metadata om deltagarna finns med, bl. a med uppgifter om deltagarnas ålder, kön, utbildning och yrke. I tillägg finns information om deltagarens tvåspråkighet, och färdighet i swahili, baserat på självvärdering.\n\nSyfte:\n\nDatamaterialet består av 36 ljudinspelningar med totalt 27 informanter/deltagare som alla är tvåspråkiga ngoni från två byar utanför Songea i Ruvumaregionen i Tanzania. Detta ljudmaterial består av 9 fokusgruppdiskussioner och 27 intervjuer med deltagarna. Alla medverkande har givit sitt tillstånd till att materialet kan användas för forskningsändamål.\nFokusgruppinspelningarna är totalt ca 6 timmar. Varje deltagare i dessa fokusgrupperna har dessutom intervjuats enskild efter fokusgruppinspelningen. Alla fokusgruppinspelningarna är transkriberade och sedan översatta till swahili och engelska. Kodväxling till swahili är markerad med fetstil i transkriptioner och översättningar. Intervjuerna genomfördes på swahili, ibland med inslag av ngoni om informanterna behövde det för att förstå. Den engelska översättningen av intervjuerna finns i datasetet, samt ljudinspelningen.\nProjektets syfte var att studera varför människor i Ruvuma-regionen i sydvästra Tanzania kodväxlar, dvs alternerar mellan två koder, swahili och ngoni, som är modersmål för de flesta som bor i området. En huvudfråga var om växlingen mellan ngoni och swahili är ett uttryck för att talare av ngoni inte längre kan uttrycka sig tillräckligt bra på sitt modersmål eller om deras kodväxling signalerar något annat. Kan det exempelvis vara ett sätt att uttrycka gruppidentitet - eller rent av flera identiteter samtidigt? Inom ramen för projektet låg fokus på hur ngoni-talare kodväxlar och om det i språksituationen finns processer som bidrar till bevarandet av modersmålet och om hur ngonitalarens attityder påverkar språkanvändning.\nI inspelningarna talat ngoni , de flesta självhushållsbönde, på ngoni om kulturarv och förändringar. Teman som diskuteras är bl. a ritualer, regler och traditioner, samt namngivning.\n\nDatamaterialet består av 36 ljudinspelningar med totalt 27 informanter/deltagare som alla är tvåspråkiga ngoni (ngoni-swahili) från två byar utanför Songea i Ruvumaregionen i Tanzania, Mhepai och Peramiho B. Detta ljudmaterial består av 9 fokusgruppdiskussioner och 27 intervjuer med deltagarna i fokusgruppsamtalen. Alla medverkande har givit sitt tillstånd till att materialet kan användas för forskningsändamål.\nFokusgruppinspelningarna utgör totalt ca 6 timmar. De 9 fokusgrupperna bestod av: 15 unga mellan 15-20 år (6 unga män och 3 unga kvinnor från Peramiho B samt 3 unga män och 3 unga kvinnor från Mhepai, och 12 personer äldre än 60år (3 äldre kvinnor och tre äldre män från varje by).\n\nVarje deltagare i dessa fokusgrupper har dessutom intervjuats enskild efter fokusgruppinspelningen. Alla fokusgruppinspelningarna är transkriberade och sedan översatta till swahili och engelska. Kodväxling till swahili är markerad med fetstil i transkriptioner och översättningar. Intervjuerna genomfördes på swahili, ibland med inslag av ngoni om informanterna behövde det för att förstå. Den engelska översättningen av intervjuerna finns i datasetet, samt ljudinspelningen. Inspelningarna gjordes in augusti-september 2014.\nEn Excel-fil med anonymiserad metadata om deltagarna finns med, bl. a med uppgifter om deltagarnas ålder, kön, utbildning och yrke. I tillägg finns information om deltagarens tvåspråkighet, och färdighet i swahili, baserat på självvärdering.','Språkligt utanförskap - identitetens betydelse i ett utvecklingsperspektiv','https://doi.org/10.5878/ekw9-mk51',1,1053,2,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'https://snd.gu.se',NULL),(681,NULL,'EstQA Question Answering dataset','https://vlo.clarin.eu/record/http_58__47__47_hdl.handle.net_47_11297_47_0-00-0000-0000-0000-014B1L?4&q=question+answering&index=0&count=5818',1,1062,0,'HTTP 200/','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(682,NULL,'Question Answering dataset - “Czy wiesz”','https://vlo.clarin.eu/record/https_58__47__47_hdl.handle.net_47_11321_47_39_64_format_61_cmdi?6&q=question+answering&index=1&count=5818',1,1062,1,'HTTP 200/','text/html',1,NULL,NULL),(683,NULL,'world war','http://catalog.paradisec.org.au/repository/DD1/091',1,1063,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(684,NULL,'3rd person description of war','https://catalog.paradisec.org.au/collections/DD1/items/265',1,1063,1,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',1,NULL,NULL),(685,NULL,'WMT18 Quality Estimation Shared Task Training and Development Data - word_level_training','https://lindat.mff.cuni.cz/repository/xmlui/bitstream/handle/11372/LRT-2619/word_level_training.tar.gz?sequence=2&isAllowed=n',1,1064,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(686,NULL,'eltec18400','https://distantreading.github.io/ELTeC/eng/ENG18400.html',1,1063,2,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',2,NULL,NULL),(687,NULL,'eltec18400','https://distantreading.github.io/ELTeC/eng/ENG18400.html',1,1063,2,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',2,NULL,NULL),(688,NULL,'Prueba','https://corpora.uni-hamburg.de/hzsk/de/islandora/object/spoken-corpus:hacaspa',1,1065,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,NULL,NULL),(689,NULL,'Czech-English Parallel Corpus 1.0 (CzEng 1.0) / data-plaintext-format.0.tar','https://lindat.mff.cuni.cz/repository/xmlui/bitstream/handle/11234/1-1458/data-plaintext-format.0.tar?sequence=4&isAllowed=y',1,1064,0,'HTTP 200/OK','application/x-tar',0,NULL,NULL),(690,NULL,'WMT18 phrase level test','https://lindat.mff.cuni.cz/repository/xmlui/bitstream/handle/11372/LRT-2805/phrase_level_test.tar.gz?sequence=3&isAllowed=n',1,1064,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',1,NULL,NULL),(691,'The corpus is available in Kielipankki - the Language Bank of Finland\'s download service. NB: The data will be reorganized in smaller, more userfriendly packages in the near future.\n\nThis subcorpus is part of the Corpus of Finnish Sign Language collected in the CFINSL project. The subcorpus comprises elicited narratives from 21 Finnish Sign Language signers who belong to different age groups and live in different parts of Finland. The material covers three fixed tasks performed by the signers: narrating about short cartoon strips, narrating about a video, and narrating a story from a picture book (The Snowman or Frog, Where are you?).  \n\nThe material consists of video files and the annotations of the videos in ELAN format as well as the metadata about the signers and the content and format of the videos. All of the video data (15,25 hours x six camera angles) has been annotated for signs and translations.\n\nThe corpus is a downloadable version of the corpus available in LAT in Kielipankki, The Language Bank of Finland.','Corpus of Finnish Sign Language: elicited narratives, Download version','http://urn.fi/urn:nbn:fi:lb-2019092710',1,1066,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(692,'Until November 2020, this corpus version is available via the LAT platform in Kielipankki - the Language Bank of Finland (see Access location).\n\nIMPORTANT NOTICE: The LAT service of the Language Bank of Finland will be discontinued in November 2020, after which this corpus version can no longer be used. However, a downloadable version of the same content will be maintained (see Relations). Further details about the most recent version are available on the info page of this corpus family (https://www.kielipankki.fi/aineistot/cfinsl). \n\nThis subcorpus is part of the Corpus of Finnish Sign Language collected in the CFINSL project. The subcorpus comprises elicited narratives from 21 Finnish Sign Language signers who belong to different age groups and live in different parts of Finland. The material covers three fixed tasks performed by the signers: narrating about short cartoon strips, narrating about a video, and narrating a story from a picture book (The Snowman or Frog, Where are you?).  \n\nThe material consists of video files and the annotations of the videos in ELAN format as well as the metadata about the signers and the content and format of the videos. All of the video data (15,25 hours x six camera angles) has been annotated for signs and translations.','Corpus of Finnish Sign Language: elicited narratives','http://urn.fi/urn:nbn:fi:lb-2019012322',1,1066,1,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(693,'Until November 2020, this corpus is available via the LAT platform in the Language Bank of Finland, see Access location.\n\nThis corpus can no longer be accessed via the LAT interface, since The LAT service of the Language Bank of Finland was discontinued in November 2020. However, the same content is available for download (see under Relations). Further details about the most recent version are available on the info page of this corpus family (https://www.kielipankki.fi/aineistot/kipo). \n\nThe language policy program of the sign languages of Finland was written in Finnish first and then translated into the Finnish sign language by two persons who speak it as their mother tongue. However some parts of the program have been produced differently. For example the preface was first produced in sign language and then translated into Finnish.\n\nMore information: http://www.kl-deaf.fi/fi-FI/kielipoliittinen-ohjelma','The Kipo Corpus (2010 The Language Policy Programme for the National Sign Languages in Finland)','http://urn.fi/urn:nbn:fi:lb-2014073031',1,1066,2,'HTTP 200/OK','text/html',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(735,'The aim of this project was the digitisation of historical language and dialect maps of the (Central) European area from 1924-1956 and the collection of historical information on these maps in a database. The historical data form the basis for a critical examination of the maps, i.e. for a historical contextualisation. Thus, the database contains information on the authors or draughtsmen of the maps, the institutional background of their creation, the year of origin of the maps, the publisher who published them. Both the scans of the maps and the data mentioned are thus accessible in the form of a digital visualisation.\nThe Project Austrian Dialect Cartography 1924-1956. Digitisation, contextualisation, visualisation is a cooperative project of the \"Wörterbuch der bairischen Mundndarten in Österreich\" (WBÖ) of the department \"Variation und Wandel des Deutschen in Österreich\" (VaWADiÖ) of the Austrian Centre for Digital Humanities (ACDH) at the Austrian Academy of Sciences and a satellite project of the special research area \"Deutsch in Österreich\" (DiÖ) of the Universities of Vienna, Graz and Salzburg and the Austrian Academy of Sciences.','DIAUMA - Digitizing Austrian Dialect Maps','https://hdl.handle.net/21.11115/0000-000D-D708-8',1,1070,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/turtle',0,'https://arche.acdh.oeaw.ac.at',NULL),(736,'Theodor Kübler war Pastor einer deutsch-protestantischen Gemeinde in London und in den Führungsgremien des German Hospitals aktiv. 1896 widmete er der langjährigen Oberschwester des Hospitals, Christiane Bürger, ein Gedicht zu deren 50. Dienstjubiläum.','Theodor Kübler: Jubiläums-Gedicht.txt','https://vlo.clarin.eu/data/clarin/results/cmdi/DARIAH_DE_Repository/hdl_21_11113_0000_000C_6ECC_4.xml',1,1070,1,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(737,'ReadMe zur Ordnerstruktur','ReadMe.txt','https://vlo.clarin.eu/data/clarin/results/cmdi/DARIAH_DE_Repository/hdl_21_11113_0000_000E_75B7_0.xml',1,1070,2,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(738,NULL,'Stifter_Bergmilch.txt','https://vlo.clarin.eu/data/clarin/results/cmdi/DARIAH_DE_Repository/hdl_21_11113_0000_000E_7553_1.xml',1,1070,3,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(739,NULL,'Eckstein_Der Leutturm von Livorno.txt','https://vlo.clarin.eu/data/clarin/results/cmdi/DARIAH_DE_Repository/hdl_21_11113_0000_000E_754B_B.xml',1,1070,4,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(740,NULL,'Montemajor_Der Gefangene.txt','https://vlo.clarin.eu/data/clarin/results/cmdi/DARIAH_DE_Repository/hdl_21_11113_0000_000E_7551_3.xml',1,1070,5,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(741,NULL,'Storm_Eine Malerarbeit.txt','https://vlo.clarin.eu/data/clarin/results/cmdi/DARIAH_DE_Repository/hdl_21_11113_0000_000E_7554_0.xml',1,1070,6,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(742,NULL,'Ungern-Sternberg_Die Doppelgaengerin.txt','https://vlo.clarin.eu/data/clarin/results/cmdi/DARIAH_DE_Repository/hdl_21_11113_0000_000E_7555_F.xml',1,1070,7,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(743,NULL,'Grillparzer_Der arme Spielmann.txt','https://vlo.clarin.eu/data/clarin/results/cmdi/DARIAH_DE_Repository/hdl_21_11113_0000_000E_754F_7.xml',1,1070,8,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(744,NULL,'Raabe_Die schwarze Galeere.txt','https://vlo.clarin.eu/data/clarin/results/cmdi/DARIAH_DE_Repository/hdl_21_11113_0000_000E_7552_2.xml',1,1070,9,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(745,NULL,'Droste-Huelshoff_Die Judenbuche.txt','https://vlo.clarin.eu/data/clarin/results/cmdi/DARIAH_DE_Repository/hdl_21_11113_0000_000E_754C_A.xml',1,1070,10,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(747,NULL,'The squire\'s story','https://hdl.handle.net/20.500.12024/3112@format=cmdi',1,1072,0,'HTTP 200/OK','application/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(748,NULL,'The old nurse\'s story','https://hdl.handle.net/20.500.12024/3108@format=cmdi',1,1072,1,'HTTP 200/OK','application/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(749,NULL,'A ghost story / by Mark Twain','https://hdl.handle.net/20.500.12024/1856@format=cmdi',1,1072,2,'HTTP 200/OK','application/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(750,NULL,'Bartleby, the scrivener: a story of Wall-street','https://hdl.handle.net/20.500.12024/3146@format=cmdi',1,1072,3,'HTTP 200/OK','application/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(751,NULL,'Bartleby, the scrivener : a story of Wall-street / by Herman Melville','https://hdl.handle.net/20.500.12024/2088@format=cmdi',1,1072,4,'HTTP 200/OK','application/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(752,'General information: \n                         The corpus contains read German speech of 179 different speakers (50 female, 129 male). Each speaker has read randomly selected sentences from four\n                          text collections: Wikipedia, the Europarl Corpus,a list of German Command/Control sentences, a corpus of web-crawled sentences that represent direct speech. \n                         The recording took place at the Language Technology and Telecooperation labs, TU-Darmstadt, Germany in 2014-2015. \n                         The task for the speaker was to read fluently and precise (no dialectal variation). Up to 5 microphones were recorded in parallel: \n                         Kinect 1 Beamformed Audio signal through Kinect SDK, Kinect 1 Direct Access as normal microphone, Internal Realtek Mic of Asus PC - near noisy fan, Samson C01U, Yamaha PSG-01S. \n                         Distance to mouth for all microphones was approx. 100cm. Room: \'dry\' acoustics (\'quiet office\'), no noise. Sampling rate: 16kHz, resolution: 16 Bit.\n                         The speech data was collected in a controlled environment (same room, same microphone distances, etc.). \n                         Each recording has a xml transcription file that also includes speaker meta data. \n                         The data is curated (manually checked and corrected), to reduce errors and artefacts. \n                         The speech data is divided into three independent data sets: Training / Test / Dev, Test and Dev contains new sentences and new speakers \n                         that are not part of training set, in order to assess model quality in a speaker-independent open-vocabulary setting. \n                         Information about the data collection procedure: \n                         (1) Train set (recordings in 2014): \n                         Sentences were randomly chosen from German Wikipedia and Europarl Corpus, to be read by the speakers. \n                         The Europarl corpus (Release v7) is a collection of the proceedings of the European Parliament between 1996 and 2011, generated by Philipp Koehn \n                         (Europarl: A Parallel Corpus for Statistical Machine Translation, Philipp Koehn, MT Summit 2005, http://www.statmt.org/europarl/). \n                         As third data set, German command and control sentences, were manually specified and would be typical for a command and control setting in living rooms.\n                         (2) Test/dev set (recordings in 2015): \n                         Additional sentences from the German Wikipedia and from the Europarl Corpus have selected for the recordings. Additionally, we collected German sentences \n                         from the web by crawling the German top-level-domain and applying language filtering and deduplification. Exclusively sentences starting with quotation marks \n                         were selected and randomly sampled. The three text sources are represented with approximately equal amounts of recordings in the test/dev set.','BAS Edition of German Distant Speech Data Corpus 2014/2015','http://hdl.handle.net/11022/1009-0000-0007-F5CB-0@format=cmdi',1,1073,0,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(753,'This corpus examines the pronunciation of different genderneutral forms in German. Various source texts were used, like newspaper articles, websites, etc.','Spoken production of gender-neutral nouns in German','http://hdl.handle.net/11022/1009-0000-0003-FF39-F@format=cmdi',1,1073,1,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(754,'FORMTASK is a telephone speech database of prompted descriptions of typical forms found in everyday life. The forms are\n			  - Berlin public transport ticket\n			  - Invoice\n			  - Austrian parking ticket\n			  - Newsstand receipt\n			  - Money transfer form\n			  To elicit a description of the forms, the following four questions were asked:\n			  1) What type of form is this?\n			  2) What date is on the form?\n			  3) What amout is on the form?\n			  4) Where is the amount written on the form?\n			  The speakers saw the form in black and white print on their personal prompt sheet on paper. Starting from version 2.1, FORMTASK is distributed as an emuR compatible emuDB corpus.','BAS FORMTASK','http://hdl.handle.net/11022/1009-0000-0005-8535-9@format=cmdi',1,1073,2,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(755,'Large set of 2274 recordings (approx. 360h) of spoken dialectal German (Saxonian) recorded in Transilvania (Romania) in approx. 250 different locations. This up-to-now unpublished material has been collected on analog tape in the 1960s and 70s by different linguists based at the universities of Bukarest, Hermannstadt and Klausenburg. Later these tapes have been digitized, and in 2009 - with the kind support of Prof. Dr. Stefan Sienerth, director of the \'Institut für deutsche Kultur und Geschichte Südosteuropas (IKGS)\' transferred to the Institute for Romance Studies (Prof. Thomas Krefeld; http://www.romanistik.uni-muenchen.de/personen/professoren/krefeld/index.html) and the LMU Center for Digital Humanities (IT-Gruppe Geisteswissenschaften [ITG; http://www.itg.lmu.de]; Dr. Stephan Lücke, Emma Mages) respectively, both of the Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München (LMU). The corpus comprises different recording strategies and discourse types: on the one hand the classic German \'Wenker\' sentences, on the other hand also fairy tales, song texts and free story-telling. Insofar, the corpus not only provides historical linguistic data but also input for ethnographical and historical disciplins. Since the age of the informants varies over a large range, this gives another dimension reflected in the metadata of the corpus. Further corpus features: geo reference of all recording sites; phonetic transcription of \'Wenker\' sentence recordings; orthographic transcription of spontaneous speech recordings (approx. 450.000 words); (partly) phonetic transcription of spontaneous speech; semantic labelling (\'ontology\'); extension to middle Bavarian recordings from the area \'Wassertal/Oberwischau\'. The ASD corpus can also be accessed at http://www.asd.gwi.uni-muenchen.de/, a dedicated website providing numerous kinds of tools, analytic approaches and visualisation. In 2016 the present corpus version was created and re-validated at the BAS CLARIN center of the Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München for indefinite achivation and distribution.','Audioatlas Siebenbuergisch-Saechsischer Dialekte','http://hdl.handle.net/11022/1009-0000-0001-27B9-3@format=cmdi',1,1073,3,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(756,'The VERIF1DE database is a subset of the VERIDAT speaker verification database collected by T-Nova. VERIDAT contains additional items and re-recordings of missing, corrupted, or otherwise unusable files in VERIF1DE. Please refer to the file DESIGN.PDF in the documentation package of this corpus for a detailed description of VERIF1DE. Users of this speech corpus are required to report any scientific publications based on these data to Felix Burkhardt (Felix.Burkhardt@telekom.de).','BAS VERIF1DE','http://hdl.handle.net/11022/1009-0000-0000-DF76-1@format=cmdi',1,1073,4,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(757,'Speech synthesis using concatenative techniques is maturing to a point where standard procedures are being implemented in a variety of products. However, because of the considerable costs most small and medium-sized companies as well as university labs cannot afford to produce the required speech resources on their own. Although there are some public domain German diphone voices available for research purposes (e.g. MBROLA) there is definitely a lack of publicly available synthesis resources. The BITS synthesis corpus (recorded and) produced by BAS fills the obvious gap.','BAS Infrastructures for Technical Speech Processing','http://hdl.handle.net/11022/1009-0000-0007-C2C0-4@format=cmdi',1,1073,5,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(758,'Collection from literature: known and documented pronunciation variants from the canonical phonological pronunciation of German in form of non-recursive re-write rules','BAS PHONRUL German Phonological Pronunciation Ruleset','http://hdl.handle.net/11022/1009-0000-0007-E7E5-2',1,1073,6,'HTTP 200/OK','text/xml',0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(767,'This study constitutes a basic description of the phonetics and phonology of the Gutnish language which is spoken in the Swedish province Gotland. Based on original recordings collected by me in Gotland during the summer of 2011 the vowel and consonant inventory is mapped, phonotactic rules are established and stress and pitch accents patterns described. The introduction gives some background information about the language and tries to evaluate the linguistic situation in Gotland. Throughout, local variation is acknowledged in an attempt to show the diversity but also the instability of the language.\n\nPurpose:\n\nThe aim of this study was to gather sound recordings of speakers of Gutnish and to use them to describe phonteic and phonological traits of the Gutnish language. I also wanted to provide some comments on the linguistic status of Gutnish.','Documentation of Gutnish phonetics and phonology','https://snd.gu.se/en/catalogue/study/ext0145',0,1077,0,NULL,NULL,0,'https://snd.gu.se',NULL),(768,'The aim of this project was the digitisation of historical language and dialect maps of the (Central) European area from 1924-1956 and the collection of historical information on these maps in a database. The historical data form the basis for a critical examination of the maps, i.e. for a historical contextualisation. Thus, the database contains information on the authors or draughtsmen of the maps, the institutional background of their creation, the year of origin of the maps, the publisher who published them. Both the scans of the maps and the data mentioned are thus accessible in the form of a digital visualisation.\nThe Project Austrian Dialect Cartography 1924-1956. Digitisation, contextualisation, visualisation is a cooperative project of the \"Wörterbuch der bairischen Mundndarten in Österreich\" (WBÖ) of the department \"Variation und Wandel des Deutschen in Österreich\" (VaWADiÖ) of the Austrian Centre for Digital Humanities (ACDH) at the Austrian Academy of Sciences and a satellite project of the special research area \"Deutsch in Österreich\" (DiÖ) of the Universities of Vienna, Graz and Salzburg and the Austrian Academy of Sciences.','DIAUMA - Digitizing Austrian Dialect Maps','https://hdl.handle.net/21.11115/0000-000D-D708-8',1,1077,0,NULL,NULL,0,'https://arche.acdh.oeaw.ac.at',NULL),(769,'The Europarl parallel corpus is extracted from the proceedings of the European Parliament. It includes versions in 21 European languages: Romanic (French, Italian, Spanish, Portuguese, Romanian), Germanic (English, Dutch, German, Danish, Swedish), Slavik (Bulgarian, Czech, Polish, Slovak, Slovene), Finni-Ugric (Finnish, Hungarian, Estonian), Baltic (Latvian, Lithuanian), and Greek. \n\nThe goal of the extraction and processing was to generate sentence aligned text for statistical machine translation systems. For this purpose we extracted matching items and labeled them with corresponding document IDs. Using a preprocessor we identified sentence boundaries. We sentence aligned the data using a tool based on the Church and Gale algorithm.','Europarl Parallel Corpus','http://urn.fi/urn:nbn:fi:lb-20140730195',0,1077,2,NULL,NULL,0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(770,'Texts in 107 languages from the W2C corpus (http://hdl.handle.net/11858/00-097C-0000-0022-6133-9), first 1,000,000 tokens per language, tagged by the delexicalized tagger described in Yu et al. (2016, LREC, Portorož, Slovenia).\r\n\r\nChanges in version 1.1: \r\n\r\n1. Universal Dependencies tagset instead of the older and smaller Google Universal POS tagset. \r\n\r\n2. SVM classifier trained on Universal Dependencies 1.2 instead of HamleDT 2.0. \r\n\r\n3. Balto-Slavic languages, Germanic languages and Romance languages were tagged by classifier trained only on the respective group of languages. Other languages were tagged by a classifier trained on all available languages. The \"c7\" combination from version 1.0 is no longer used.','Deltacorpus 1.1','https://hdl.handle.net/11234/1-1743@format=cmdi',0,1077,3,NULL,NULL,0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(771,'The Europarl parallel corpus is extracted from the proceedings of the European Parliament. It includes versions in 21 European languages: Romanic (French, Italian, Spanish, Portuguese, Romanian), Germanic (English, Dutch, German, Danish, Swedish), Slavik (Bulgarian, Czech, Polish, Slovak, Slovene), Finni-Ugric (Finnish, Hungarian, Estonian), Baltic (Latvian, Lithuanian), and Greek. \n\nThe goal of the extraction and processing was to generate sentence aligned text for statistical machine translation systems. For this purpose we extracted matching items and labeled them with corresponding document IDs. Using a preprocessor we identified sentence boundaries. We sentence aligned the data using a tool based on the Church and Gale algorithm.','Europarl Parallel Corpus','http://urn.fi/urn:nbn:fi:lb-20140730195',0,1077,1,NULL,NULL,0,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(772,'Texts in 107 languages from the W2C corpus (http://hdl.handle.net/11858/00-097C-0000-0022-6133-9), first 1,000,000 tokens per language, tagged by the delexicalized tagger described in Yu et al. (2016, LREC, Portorož, Slovenia).\r\n\r\nChanges in version 1.1: \r\n\r\n1. Universal Dependencies tagset instead of the older and smaller Google Universal POS tagset. \r\n\r\n2. SVM classifier trained on Universal Dependencies 1.2 instead of HamleDT 2.0. \r\n\r\n3. Balto-Slavic languages, Germanic languages and Romance languages were tagged by classifier trained only on the respective group of languages. Other languages were tagged by a classifier trained on all available languages. The \"c7\" combination from version 1.0 is no longer used.','Deltacorpus 1.1','https://hdl.handle.net/11234/1-1743@format=cmdi',0,1077,3,NULL,NULL,3,'https://vlo.clarin.eu',NULL),(773,NULL,'Wikipedia page on germanic languages','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germanic_languages',1,1077,2,NULL,NULL,2,NULL,NULL),(774,NULL,'How CLARIN Enables Data to Be FAIR','https://www.clarin.eu/fair',1,1078,1,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(775,NULL,'The Open Handbook of Linguistic Data Management','https://doi.org/10.7551/mitpress/12200.001.0001',1,1078,2,NULL,NULL,2,NULL,NULL),(776,NULL,'Linguistic Data Management - Online Companion Course to the Open Handbook Linguistic Data Management','https://sites.google.com/hawaii.edu/linguisticdatamanagement',1,1078,3,NULL,NULL,3,NULL,NULL),(777,NULL,'Intellectual Property Rights and Legal Issues with CMC Corpora','https://www.clarin.eu/sites/default/files/kamocki-IPR_and_legal_issues.pdf',1,1078,6,NULL,NULL,6,NULL,NULL),(778,NULL,'Data Formats and Standards for Interoperable CMC Corpora','https://www.clarin.eu/sites/default/files/luengen_beisswenger-standards_and_data_formats.pdf',1,1078,7,NULL,NULL,7,NULL,NULL),(779,NULL,'Introduction to CMC  Metadata and Language Data  Repositories','https://www.clarin.eu/sites/default/files/koenig-metadata_and_repositories.pdf',1,1078,5,NULL,NULL,5,NULL,NULL),(780,NULL,'Introduction to Data Management & the FAIR Guiding Principles for Data Stewardship','https://www.clarin.eu/sites/default/files/stemle_frey-data_managment_and_fair.pdf',1,1078,4,NULL,NULL,4,NULL,NULL),(781,NULL,'Jong, Franciska de, Bente Maegaard, Koenraad De Smedt, Darja Fišer & Dieter Van Uytvanck (2018)','http://hdl.handle.net/1874/364776',1,1078,8,NULL,NULL,8,NULL,NULL),(782,NULL,'CLARIN and FAIR Principles','https://youtu.be/FQVkNQ9CxYs',1,1078,0,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL),(783,NULL,'PARTHENOS Guidelines to FAIRify data management and make data reusable','https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.2668478',1,1078,9,NULL,NULL,9,NULL,NULL),(784,NULL,'WebLicht Getting Started','ht